//
// A CONVERSION AND AN INSTANTIATION ARE NOT RANKED (`@FR-D-Rank`, refused by
// `@FR-Disp-Ambiguous`): GENERIC is INCOMPARABLE with CONVERTED, so a call that one member takes
// by an implicit conversion (`character` into `integer`) and another by instantiating its type
// variable is refused, naming both — the language refuses rather than choosing.  The rule's own
// example is `float` against `T` with an integer (in the attic, `1925b`); this is the other
// conversion family, character into integer, so the incomparability is the rank's and not the
// one pair's.  One refusal per file.
//
// @falsified-at: none — a conformance guard; the refusal fired as expected on 18594597b,
//   interpret and native.
// CHANNEL: the compile (the expected diagnostic).
// ARMED: no instrument.
// WITNESS: the diagnostic names both members.
// HOLDS: an `integer` argument (EXACT) is not refused — the sibling cell's `rg(integer)` takes it.
// @EXPECT_ERROR: `rc(character)` is ambiguous — it is taken by rc(integer) and rc<T>(T)

fn rc(x: integer) -> text { "integer {x}" }
fn rc<T>(_x: T) -> text { "generic" }

fn main() {
  c = 'A';
  println(rc(c));
}
